  "client_view_text": "  When do you use a write-ahead log?\n\n## Step 1: Understanding the Basics of a Write-Ahead Log\nA write-ahead log (WAL) is a mechanism used in databases to ensure data integrity and allow for recovery after a system crash. It works by temporarily storing database operations in a log file before applying them to the database's main memory or storage.\n\n## Step 2: How a Write-Ahead Log Works\nWhen a transaction (a series of database operations) is initiated, the database first writes the operations to the log file before executing them on the database itself. This ensures that even if a crash occurs before the transaction is fully committed, the log file contains a record of all operations that were attempted. This allows the database to recover to a consistent state by replaying the operations from the log file.\n\n## Step 3: Recovery Process After a Crash\nIn the event of a crash, the database can recover by reading through the log file from the last checkpoint (where the database had previously confirmed all operations up to that point were committed successfully) up to the point of the crash. For each operation in the log, the database checks if the operation has been successfully applied to the database's main memory or storage. If not, it reapplies"
